, 41.354, 41.424, 41.459, 41.517, 41.552, 41.679, 41.749, 41.819, 41.877, 41.9, 41.935, 41.97, 42.005, 42.039, 42.086, 42.132, 42.179, 42.237, 42.306, 42.353, 42.411, 42.481, 42.597, 42.655, 42.713, 42.748, 42.794, 42.852, 42.899, 42.945, 42.968, 43.026, 43.049, 43.108, 43.142, 43.2, 43.235, 43.293, 43.328, 43.386, 43.456, 43.525, 43.56, 43.607, 43.642, 43.688, 43.723, 43.781, 43.839, 43.885, 43.932, 43.99, 44.06, 44.129, 44.245, 44.443]}, "quality_check": null, "script_sha256": "b98394cba765f5d144c113e1a09f8c6d910473547aaa6d50863e20289f96c74f", "text": "By August 2021, he was being asked about another summer of spending. Klopp contrasted Liverpool's approach with the financial resources of their rivals. Liverpool spent money too. A fair account of this rivalry has to acknowledge that. Klopp was not running a tiny club with no resources, and spending more does not itself establish a rule breach. His argument concerned the boundaries: what a club could spend, where that money came from, and whether the limits applied consistently. In October 2022, before facing City, he made the contrast particularly sharp. City could add Haaland to an already outstanding team. Liverpool could not simply copy that approach whenever a new problem appeared.", "voice_id": "bu5eKETbFKC8G702EAU4", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2"}